<div style="background:#fff;"><div class="cap-section"><div style="margin-bottom:8px;"><div style="margin-bottom:26px;"><h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:28px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0;">Frequently asked questions</h2></div>
    <div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:var(--radius-card);overflow:hidden;background:#fff;">
      <details class="sc-faq" open>
      <summary>When does COB make more sense than a packaged part?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">COB is useful where assembly height, board area or unit cost at volume are important. Removing the separate IC package reduces stack height and package cost, making the process well suited to displays, sensors, RFID and other compact modules.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>What wire and pitch can you bond?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">Hana supports gold ball bonding from 18 µm wire and aluminum wedge bonding for power devices, with bond pitch down to 60 µm. Bonds are pull- and shear-tested to MIL-STD-883 methods.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Can COB and SMT run on the same board?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">Yes. Surface-mount components and chip-on-board die can be assembled on the same PCB, with the COB process integrated into the overall manufacturing flow.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>What quality systems cover COB?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">ISO 9001 applies group-wide, with IATF 16949 for automotive work and ISO 13485 for medical. Bonding runs in cleanroom-controlled, ESD-managed areas meeting ANSI/ESD S20.20.</div>
    </details>
      <details class="sc-faq">
      <summary>Do you protect customer designs and IP?<span class="sc-faq-mk">+</span></summary>
      <div class="sc-faq-a">An NDA is signed before die, BOM or CAD files are shared. Build areas are access-controlled, and programs can be assigned to dedicated lines where required.</div>
    </details>
    </div>
